WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:11.462
This is Chapter 9: drift pipelines
and evidence to the ISSO.

4
00:00:11.462 --> 00:00:15.063
A check that runs when someone
remembers is a spot check.

5
00:00:15.063 --> 00:00:18.676
A check that runs every day,
says what moved since yesterday,

6
00:00:18.676 --> 00:00:23.175
and leaves evidence nobody can edit
afterward is continuous monitoring.

7
00:00:23.175 --> 00:00:28.298
We'll start from Learn's sample workflows
for signing in from GitHub Actions without

8
00:00:28.298 --> 00:00:31.739
a secret, see what they leave
out, then walk the comparison,

9
00:00:31.739 --> 00:00:35.325
the findings, the evidence
package, two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:35.325 --> 00:00:40.840
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.115
Why build a pipeline at all?

12
00:00:46.115 --> 00:00:51.001
Because three earlier books already say
what continuous monitoring must produce:

13
00:00:51.001 --> 00:00:55.250
drift measured against a versioned
record, no closure without its proof,

14
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:58.578
and a finding closed only when
a later look shows it fixed.

15
00:00:58.578 --> 00:01:03.090
Each validation kit check already compares
the tenant with the program's record.

16
00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.565
The pipeline adds one more comparison:
today's run against yesterday's.

17
00:01:07.565 --> 00:01:08.890
That finds change.

18
00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:11.340
And it puts the closing rule into code.

19
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.053
A finding is resolved
only if the same check

20
00:01:14.053 --> 00:01:16.390
read the tenant again and didn't see it.

21
00:01:16.390 --> 00:01:18.730
A check that errored proves nothing.

22
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.893
Why GitHub Actions?

23
00:01:23.893 --> 00:01:26.966
Learn documents two ways
to run a scheduled job

24
00:01:26.966 --> 00:01:29.804
that signs in to Azure
with no stored secret.

25
00:01:29.804 --> 00:01:32.792
The chapter picks GitHub
Actions for three reasons.

26
00:01:32.792 --> 00:01:36.045
Learn publishes complete
sample workflows for it,

27
00:01:36.045 --> 00:01:38.368
including the US Government sign-in.

28
00:01:38.368 --> 00:01:42.530
The sign-in action is Microsoft's
own, with its source published.

29
00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.922
And for the other option, Azure Pipelines,

30
00:01:44.922 --> 00:01:48.017
the automatic setup doesn't
work in Azure Government.

31
00:01:48.017 --> 00:01:50.083
For this estate either would do,

32
00:01:50.083 --> 00:01:54.692
but one path that also documents the
higher clouds is easier to hand on.

33
00:01:54.692 --> 00:01:58.560
And the comparator and packager
work the same under either.

34
00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.738
Which cloud matters at every step:
the sign-in authority, the Graph host,

35
00:02:06.738 --> 00:02:09.869
the Azure environment,
the token audience and the

36
00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:12.923
Graph PowerShell environment
all have to agree.

37
00:02:12.923 --> 00:02:15.303
For a GCC tenant like this estate's,

38
00:02:15.303 --> 00:02:18.959
Learn says apps keep using
the worldwide endpoints.

39
00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:23.436
The higher clouds share a government
sign-in authority, and here's the trap:

40
00:02:23.436 --> 00:02:26.665
the Azure sign-in environment
is the same for GCC High

41
00:02:26.665 --> 00:02:29.747
and the Defense cloud, but
their Graph hosts differ.

42
00:02:29.747 --> 00:02:34.073
So the Graph host must come from the
cloud profile, never from the sign-in.

43
00:02:34.073 --> 00:02:37.380
An unknown cloud name is refused outright.

44
00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.692
Here's what Learn gives you:
one page with three workflows.

45
00:02:44.692 --> 00:02:47.625
One signs in and runs
a single Azure command,

46
00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:51.455
one adds PowerShell, and one
signs in to Azure Government.

47
00:02:51.455 --> 00:02:54.141
Each job asks for exactly one permission,

48
00:02:54.141 --> 00:02:58.880
the right to request a sign-in token,
and keeps its identifiers in secrets.

49
00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.196
Before any of it works,

50
00:03:00.196 --> 00:03:04.143
the app needs a federated credential
that trusts the repository.

51
00:03:04.143 --> 00:03:07.213
Those workflows only
exist inside the web page,

52
00:03:07.213 --> 00:03:11.307
so the repository pins the page
and the action's own definition,

53
00:03:11.307 --> 00:03:15.480
and a linter checks each workflow
offline against twelve rules.

54
00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.980
Nothing runs, nothing signs in.

55
00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:26.230
Checking the samples offline shows
what they do well, and the pipeline

56
00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:30.343
keeps all of it: the same sign-in,
the same secrets, the single grant.

57
00:03:30.343 --> 00:03:32.632
What they leave out starts with timing:

58
00:03:32.632 --> 00:03:36.855
they run when someone pushes a commit,
and drift doesn't wait for a commit.

59
00:03:36.855 --> 00:03:40.127
Their actions are referenced
by tags that can be moved,

60
00:03:40.127 --> 00:03:44.780
and an older major version at that, so
the pipeline pins each action to a commit.

61
00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.238
Tool versions say latest,

62
00:03:46.238 --> 00:03:49.918
so the tool that produced the
evidence could change unreviewed.

63
00:03:49.918 --> 00:03:54.520
And the US Government sample,
copied as is, doesn't even parse.

64
00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:01.345
The rest of the gaps are
about identity and scope.

65
00:04:01.345 --> 00:04:05.855
The sample jobs name no environment,
so the token's subject changes with

66
00:04:05.855 --> 00:04:10.670
the branch or event, and a federated
credential matches one subject exactly.

67
00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:14.845
The pipeline runs in a named
environment, so the subject is fixed.

68
00:04:14.845 --> 00:04:18.036
The samples stop right
after signing in to Azure:

69
00:04:18.036 --> 00:04:20.907
no Graph token, nothing
on the higher clouds.

70
00:04:20.907 --> 00:04:23.133
They don't stop two runs overlapping.

71
00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:25.208
And after sign-in there's nothing at all.

72
00:04:25.208 --> 00:04:28.620
The verdict: use Learn's
sign-in exactly as published,

73
00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.380
pin it, schedule it, and put
everything else behind it.

74
00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:41.055
The example is two Python files, one
sample workflow and two test files.

75
00:04:41.055 --> 00:04:44.493
The workflow has two jobs with
a deliberate wall between them.

76
00:04:44.493 --> 00:04:49.430
The first can read the tenant: it signs
in, gets a Graph token for the right host,

77
00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:54.917
runs the validation kit, compares with the
previous package and writes new evidence.

78
00:04:54.917 --> 00:04:59.342
The second can write to the issue
tracker, but has no Azure access at all.

79
00:04:59.342 --> 00:05:03.130
So the identity that reads the
tenant never writes to the tracker.

80
00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:09.020
One check is delegated only, so it's
honestly listed as unverified on every run.

81
00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:17.395
Before comparing anything, the pipeline
makes sure the comparison means something.

82
00:05:17.395 --> 00:05:20.649
It checks each run against
the kit's own manifest,

83
00:05:20.649 --> 00:05:23.333
so a run edited after the fact is refused.

84
00:05:23.333 --> 00:05:28.445
Then it refuses to compare runs from
different modes or clouds, or out of order.

85
00:05:28.445 --> 00:05:30.079
The subtle one is tenants:

86
00:05:30.079 --> 00:05:33.970
a test tenant and production can
share a cloud and look alike.

87
00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:37.015
The kit records the tenant
the token was issued for,

88
00:05:37.015 --> 00:05:41.292
and the pipeline refuses to compare
two different tenants, or a run with

89
00:05:41.292 --> 00:05:45.920
no tenant recorded at all, unless
someone records a reviewed exception.

90
00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:53.333
Every difference between runs
lands in one of five results.

91
00:05:53.333 --> 00:05:55.957
New and changed findings open issues.

92
00:05:55.957 --> 00:05:59.583
A change only in the detail is
recorded but opens nothing.

93
00:05:59.583 --> 00:06:04.083
A finding is resolved only when its
check actually read the tenant this run.

94
00:06:04.083 --> 00:06:08.142
And anything from a check that
errored or didn't run is unverified,

95
00:06:08.142 --> 00:06:10.021
so earlier findings stay open.

96
00:06:10.021 --> 00:06:13.228
Each finding has a fingerprint
that's the same every run,

97
00:06:13.228 --> 00:06:15.320
so there are never duplicate issues.

98
00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:18.606
Issues carry object identifiers,
not people's names,

99
00:06:18.606 --> 00:06:23.090
because the tracker has a wider
audience than the evidence store.

100
00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:31.614
Each run becomes an evidence package: the
kit's run as written, the drift report,

101
00:06:31.614 --> 00:06:35.941
the latest provenance from the other
kits, a manifest, and a list of file

102
00:06:35.941 --> 00:06:40.640
hashes the security officer can check
with a standard tool and nothing else.

103
00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.746
Each manifest records the hash of the
previous one, so the packages form a chain:

104
00:06:45.746 --> 00:06:49.202
a replaced package or a missing
day shows up as a break.

105
00:06:49.202 --> 00:06:51.128
The packager doesn't sign, though.

106
00:06:51.128 --> 00:06:53.891
Locked retention stops
changes after the fact,

107
00:06:53.891 --> 00:06:59.010
and signing with a key the pipeline holds
is a choice left to the security officer.

108
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:06.536
The chapter records three
places where sources disagree,

109
00:07:06.536 --> 00:07:09.210
so nobody quietly loosens the code later.

110
00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:13.420
The biggest is the type of the Graph
token: the reference page says one thing,

111
00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.728
a troubleshooting article says another,

112
00:07:15.728 --> 00:07:18.648
and the module's own source
sides with the reference.

113
00:07:18.648 --> 00:07:22.748
The pipeline passes the safer form
and confirms it in the second lab.

114
00:07:22.748 --> 00:07:26.309
The other two are small: a
trailing slash on the issuer,

115
00:07:26.309 --> 00:07:29.060
and how a pull-request subject is spelled.

116
00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:34.430
The action version and the one preview
feature, which isn't used, are on the slide.

117
00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:39.230
Who's involved?

118
00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:43.128
The platform and pipeline engineers
who own the scheduled checks,

119
00:07:43.128 --> 00:07:47.805
and the security officers and reviewers
who receive the evidence and verify it.

120
00:07:47.805 --> 00:07:50.042
The permissions are deliberately split.

121
00:07:50.042 --> 00:07:52.082
A handful of administrator roles,

122
00:07:52.082 --> 00:07:55.142
or the app's owner, can add
the federated credential.

123
00:07:55.142 --> 00:07:59.406
The pipeline's identity gets only
the read permissions its checks need,

124
00:07:59.406 --> 00:08:02.080
plus write access to
one evidence container.

125
00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:07.018
And only the second job, which has no
Azure access at all, can write issues.

126
00:08:07.018 --> 00:08:11.190
No identity both reads the tenant
and writes to the tracker.

127
00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:18.427
Lab 1 runs on a laptop
with Python and no tenant.

128
00:08:18.427 --> 00:08:21.179
First, lint Learn's three
samples as published:

129
00:08:21.179 --> 00:08:25.957
the first two have several findings each,
unpinned actions and latest versions

130
00:08:25.957 --> 00:08:30.228
among them, and the Government sample
fails because it isn't valid at all.

131
00:08:30.228 --> 00:08:33.303
Then lint the chapter's own
workflow, and it's clean.

132
00:08:33.303 --> 00:08:36.637
Print the Defense cloud's
profile to see its hosts,

133
00:08:36.637 --> 00:08:39.728
and try a cloud name that
doesn't exist: refused.

134
00:08:39.728 --> 00:08:43.589
Then record the first day's run
as a baseline and package it.

135
00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:47.780
Note the manifest hash; the next
package will point back to it.

136
00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:53.992
Now compare the second day with the first.

137
00:08:53.992 --> 00:08:57.062
You'll see each kind of
result, and one matters most:

138
00:08:57.062 --> 00:09:00.355
a check whose read failed
with an access-denied error.

139
00:09:00.355 --> 00:09:04.317
Its finding isn't resolved; it's
unverified, and stays open.

140
00:09:04.317 --> 00:09:07.458
Draft the issues and search
for a person's name:

141
00:09:07.458 --> 00:09:10.268
it isn't there, only
the object identifier.

142
00:09:10.268 --> 00:09:12.866
Package the second run,
chained to the first,

143
00:09:12.866 --> 00:09:16.330
and verify it with the pipeline
and with the standard tool.

144
00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:19.843
Then tamper with one file,
and verification fails.

145
00:09:19.843 --> 00:09:22.793
Swap the runs, and the comparison refuses.

146
00:09:22.793 --> 00:09:25.720
Run the tests, and seventy-four pass.

147
00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:31.064
Lab 2 uses a test tenant,

148
00:09:31.064 --> 00:09:35.845
a test subscription and a private
test repository, never production.

149
00:09:35.845 --> 00:09:40.546
Register an app and give it a federated
credential that trusts exactly one

150
00:09:40.546 --> 00:09:44.033
environment in the repository;
Learn warns that a wrong

151
00:09:44.033 --> 00:09:47.445
subject is accepted silently
and only fails at sign-in.

152
00:09:47.445 --> 00:09:51.543
Grant only the reads, and write
access to one evidence container

153
00:09:51.543 --> 00:09:55.795
whose retention policy stays unlocked,
so the lab can be cleaned up.

154
00:09:55.795 --> 00:10:01.095
Run it by hand to set the baseline, make
one change, run again, and check the chain.

155
00:10:01.095 --> 00:10:04.500
Then let the schedule run,
and back everything out.

156
00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:13.162
The most important danger is the first:
a failed read reported as a fix.

157
00:10:13.162 --> 00:10:16.394
An access error makes
yesterday's findings disappear,

158
00:10:16.394 --> 00:10:19.051
and without a guard
their issues would close.

159
00:10:19.051 --> 00:10:20.713
Here they stay unverified.

160
00:10:20.713 --> 00:10:25.141
The rest follow from the same habit
of not trusting what you can't prove:

161
00:10:25.141 --> 00:10:30.028
refuse runs that can't be compared, keep
secrets out entirely, pin every action,

162
00:10:30.028 --> 00:10:34.991
take the Graph host from the profile, keep
reading and writing in separate jobs,

163
00:10:34.991 --> 00:10:36.824
keep names out of the tracker,

164
00:10:36.824 --> 00:10:41.380
and make the evidence unchangeable,
chained, and checked every day.

165
00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:46.480
Here's what done looks like.

166
00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.730
The workflow lints clean,
with every action pinned.

167
00:10:49.730 --> 00:10:53.609
The app signs in with a federated
credential and nothing stored,

168
00:10:53.609 --> 00:10:57.342
holds only the reads it needs,
and the cloud settings all agree.

169
00:10:57.342 --> 00:10:59.929
Reading and writing stay in separate jobs.

170
00:10:59.929 --> 00:11:05.380
Findings from a failed check stay open,
and issues carry identifiers, not names.

171
00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:10.372
Every package verifies with a standard tool
and chains to the one before, retention

172
00:11:10.372 --> 00:11:14.930
is locked in production, and someone
checks every day that a package arrived.

173
00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:17.155
That's the last chapter of the workbook.

174
00:11:17.155 --> 00:11:18.320
Thanks for watching.
